window.TEXTBOOK_UNITS.devops=[
 ['Treat DevOps as an engineered system with requirements, operating conditions, people, processes, technology, and measurable outcomes.','DevOps engineering','Deliberately designing and operating an integrated software-delivery system against explicit goals and constraints.','Why is buying a DevOps toolchain insufficient?','Tools cannot by themselves establish ownership, collaboration, compatible architecture, useful tests, security, or operational feedback. Begin with a value stream and desired outcomes, then design practices and automation around the actual constraints.'],
 ['Separate an aspirational transformation slogan from requirements, design, implementation, operation, and continuous improvement.','Value stream','The end-to-end activities and waits through which an idea or change becomes a useful outcome for its customer.','What should a DevOps transformation optimize?','Optimize useful customer outcomes and end-to-end flow while maintaining quality, security, and reliability. Local increases in developer or deployment activity can simply push work into another queue. Define the unit of work and measure waiting as well as processing.'],
 ['The book separates leadership, collaborative culture, design for DevOps, continuous integration, continuous testing, elastic infrastructure, continuous monitoring, continuous security, and continuous delivery. Cross-cutting automation and governance connect them.','CALMS','Culture, Automation, Lean, Measurement, and Sharing: complementary dimensions used to reason about DevOps practice.','How do the nine pillars prevent a tool-only transformation?','They make leadership, collaboration, design, verification, infrastructure, observation, security, and delivery visible together. Use them to identify a weak capability and its effect on the whole flow. They are the author’s framework, not a universal certification or proof of maturity.'],
 ['Connect the transformation to business and team needs rather than treating DevOps as an end product.','Outcome metric','A measure of a desired result, distinct from counting the activities performed to pursue it.','How would you justify a DevOps investment?','Describe the present bottleneck, affected customer outcome, baseline, proposed change, and measurable result. Include risk and operating cost, then validate improvement with a representative pilot rather than promising a universal productivity percentage.'],
 ['Engineer people, process, and technology together, including leadership, collaboration, change management, and operating responsibility.','Sociotechnical system','A system whose outcomes depend jointly on human organization and technical mechanisms.','Why can technically sound automation fail organizationally?','Unclear ownership, incentives, missing skills, poor handoffs, or lack of trust can prevent adoption and safe operation. Map responsibilities, involve the people using the workflow, define support, and test the process as well as its scripts.'],
 ['Value-stream management brings visibility across multiple delivery stages and streams, including waits, handoffs, and bottlenecks.','Lead time','Elapsed time from a defined start of work to a defined delivered outcome; the boundaries must be stated.','How do you find a value-stream bottleneck?','Trace one representative change end to end and measure work time, waiting, rework, and queues at each stage. Validate which constraint limits flow, improve it, and remeasure the whole stream. Do not assume the slowest-looking team or tool is the limiting constraint.'],
 ['Application release automation coordinates packaging, environments, dependencies, promotion, and release status across the lifecycle.','Release orchestration','Coordinating the ordered activities, dependencies, decisions, and evidence needed to release a change.','How does release automation differ from one deployment script?','A script may change one environment. Release orchestration connects artifacts, dependencies, approvals, environment readiness, verification, rollout, and recovery across stages. Preserve traceability and define the behavior of interrupted or partially successful steps.'],
 ['Version application code, infrastructure, pipeline definitions, dependencies, and compatible release inputs.','Immutable artifact','A build output whose content is fixed and identified so the same tested bytes can be promoted and traced.','Why not rebuild the application independently for each environment?','Dependencies, tooling, or inputs can differ and produce untested bytes. Build once from controlled inputs, identify the artifact, and promote it while managing environment configuration separately. Reproducibility and provenance require more than a Git commit label.'],
 ['Integrate security practices across design, source, builds, dependencies, tests, release, and production operation.','DevSecOps','Integrating security ownership, controls, evidence, and feedback throughout the delivery and operation lifecycle.','Where should secrets live in a pipeline?','Keep secrets in a controlled secret-management mechanism with narrowly scoped, short-lived access where possible. Do not commit them, echo them into logs, or bake them into widely distributed artifacts. Define rotation, auditing, and the trust boundary of build agents.'],
 ['A curated service catalog makes internal capabilities, ownership, access, documentation, and support discoverable.','Service catalog','A maintained inventory of available services and the information needed to request, use, support, and govern them.','What makes a service catalog useful rather than a stale list?','Record a responsible owner, supported interface, access path, lifecycle status, operational expectations, and support route. Keep entries connected to the actual service lifecycle and validate their accuracy. Discoverability without usable ownership and support provides little value.'],
 ['Governance must provide timely, proportionate decisions and evidence as change frequency increases.','Policy as code','Expressing applicable rules in versioned, testable, automatically evaluated form where appropriate, with clear ownership and exception handling.','How can governance support fast delivery?','Make common low-risk paths predictable and automate evidence collection and checks. Reserve deliberate review for the risks needing human judgment. Define exceptions, accountability, auditability, and decision latency instead of replacing every manual approval with an unexplained script.'],
 ['SRE connects service objectives, measurement, automation, capacity, incident response, and change risk.','Error budget','The permitted unreliability implied by an SLO over a defined window, used to inform reliability and delivery decisions.','How can an error budget affect release policy?','Measure the relevant SLI against its objective and use an agreed policy when reliability consumption is high. This can prioritize corrective work or reduce risky changes. Define the request population and time window; the budget is not a blanket allowance for avoidable incidents.'],
 ['Plan for disasters affecting applications, pipelines, infrastructure, credentials, people, and dependencies.','Recovery exercise','A controlled test of restoring a defined service and its data under an explicit failure scenario and objective.','Why does a successful backup job not prove recovery readiness?','Restoration may fail, omit dependencies, take too long, or violate data consistency. Test restore procedures, permissions, keys, dependencies, runbooks, and communication. Measure RPO and RTO against the agreed scenario.'],
 ['Application architecture, testability, deployability, observability, state, and dependencies shape delivery effectiveness.','Deployability','The ability to release a compatible change safely and repeatably within the system’s operating constraints.','Must an application become microservices before adopting DevOps?','No. A modular application with compatible interfaces, automated verification, clear ownership, and reliable rollout can improve delivery. Extract services when independent evolution or scaling justifies the added distributed-system costs, not merely to match a diagram.'],
 ['Engineer the pipeline as a product with versioned configuration, artifacts, stages, verification, feedback, and support.','Pipeline gate','A defined condition or decision that must be satisfied before work progresses to a later stage.','What should happen when a pipeline gate fails?','Preserve useful diagnostic evidence, stop unsafe promotion, identify ownership, and provide a repeatable retry or remediation path. Avoid bypassing checks silently or converting unreliable tests into meaningless mandatory steps. Validate the gate against the risk it is meant to control.'],
 ['Elastic infrastructure requires reproducible provisioning, capacity, isolation, configuration, lifecycle, cost, and operational feedback.','Infrastructure as code','Managing infrastructure intent through versioned, reviewable machine-readable definitions and controlled execution.','Does infrastructure as code prevent configuration drift automatically?','No. Out-of-band changes, external dependencies, provider behavior, and incomplete state can still produce divergence. Detect and reconcile drift safely, review plans, protect state and credentials, and test changes and recovery.'],
 ['Continuous testing addresses environments, data, automation, selection, feedback time, and risk throughout the pipeline.','Flaky test','A test whose outcome changes without a relevant change in the behavior it is intended to verify.','How should a team handle flaky tests?','Investigate nondeterminism, environmental dependencies, timing, and data isolation. Track ownership and fix or redesign the test. If quarantining is necessary, retain visibility and a repair deadline rather than silently removing confidence or retrying until an arbitrary pass.'],
 ['Monitor application, pipeline, infrastructure, and relevant team/process health to support actionable feedback.','Actionable alert','A notification tied to a meaningful symptom, owner, urgency, and practical response rather than raw noise.','What should trigger an on-call page?','Prefer a significant user-impacting symptom or imminent failure needing immediate action. Define ownership and a runbook, deduplicate noise, and examine the SLO and evidence. Not every dashboard threshold or individual log error deserves to wake a person.'],
 ['Distinguish the delivery pipeline, continuous delivery, and continuous deployment. Design staged release and rollback or forward recovery.','Continuous delivery','Keeping software in a state that can be released safely and repeatably on demand; production release need not be fully automatic.','Compare blue-green deployment with a canary rollout.','Blue-green switches between prepared environments, while a canary exposes a limited population before broader rollout. Both need compatible data and dependencies, meaningful verification, capacity, and an understood recovery path. Routing a binary back does not undo every data change.'],
 ['The seven-step framework links vision, alignment, assessment, solution, realization, operationalization, and expansion.','Transformation cycle','A bounded sequence of understanding, changing, validating, and operating a delivery capability, followed by further learning.','Why use a staged transformation blueprint?','It connects sponsors, requirements, evidence, design, implementation, support, and expansion instead of starting with disconnected tool purchases. Adapt scope and cadence to the organization and verify outcomes after each cycle.'],
 ['Visioning identifies sponsorship, the strategic direction, partners, goals, and commitments for the next step.','Transformation vision','A shared statement of the desired delivery outcomes and their business rationale, scope, and sponsorship.','What evidence should come out of visioning?','Identify accountable sponsors, the affected value streams, intended outcomes, scope, and a commitment to the next decision. Avoid replacing measurable intent with a slogan such as becoming the most mature DevOps organization.'],
 ['Alignment translates a leadership vision into agreed goals and participation across the relevant application teams.','Stakeholder alignment','Agreement on goals, responsibilities, constraints, and decision-making among people affected by a change.','What would you do when development and operations disagree on the goal?','Expose the customer outcome, reliability needs, constraints, and incentives behind both positions. Build an explicit shared objective and decision process. Document trade-offs and ownership rather than declaring one team the blocker.'],
 ['Assessment discovers the present application, practices, maturity evidence, and current value stream before choosing a solution.','Current-state map','A representation of actual work, queues, handoffs, feedback, and dependencies before the planned improvement.','Why assess before choosing tools?','Without a baseline and bottleneck evidence, a tool may optimize the wrong stage or impose unnecessary complexity. Observe real workflows, collect data, involve practitioners, and separate measured facts from maturity-score opinions.'],
 ['Solution design turns assessed needs into a future value stream, requirements, roadmap, and aligned backlog.','Future-state map','A proposed end-to-end workflow showing how changes are expected to address verified constraints and achieve defined goals.','How do you avoid a future-state diagram becoming wishful thinking?','Link each proposed change to a requirement, testable assumption, owner, and measurable outcome. State dependencies, cost, risk, sequencing, and how a pilot will validate the design before wider adoption.'],
 ['Realization builds and validates the chosen DevOps solution rather than treating implementation as an unstructured script collection.','Pilot','A deliberately scoped implementation used to test assumptions and operating behavior before broader rollout.','What should a DevOps pilot prove?','Demonstrate the complete useful path with representative users, changes, data, support, security, and failure recovery. Compare results with the baseline and goals. A successful demo without operational evidence is not sufficient proof for organization-wide adoption.'],
 ['Operationalization establishes access, governance, monitoring, support, training, and evolution for a deployed solution.','Operational ownership','Explicit responsibility for service behavior, support, changes, risks, and recovery throughout its useful life.','What must be ready before handing a pipeline to other teams?','Provide supported interfaces, onboarding, runbooks, access controls, monitoring, incident and support paths, capacity, and change ownership. Confirm users can operate it under realistic failures, not just execute the happy-path example.'],
 ['Expansion shares proven practices and adapts them to more applications, pipeline variations, and regions.','Yokoten','Sharing and adapting useful improvement knowledge across contexts rather than blindly copying one implementation.','When is it safe to expand a DevOps solution?','Validate the pilot’s outcomes and operating model, identify differences in the next context, and stage adoption with feedback and support. Preserve the principle that worked while testing the assumptions that may not transfer.'],
 ['The future-oriented chapter encourages evolution beyond a single improvement cycle. Treat predictions and product examples as historical context.','Continuous improvement','An ongoing process of testing changes against evidence and revisiting constraints, rather than assuming an organization has reached a final ideal state.','How do you keep continuous improvement focused?','Choose a meaningful current constraint, state a hypothesis, make a bounded change, and measure the outcome and side effects. Retain what works and stop or revise changes that do not produce the intended result.'],
 ['Continuous learning requires role-aware skill development, practice, feedback, and knowledge sharing.','Learning loop','A cycle of practice, observation, feedback, and adjusted behavior that produces demonstrated capability rather than attendance alone.','How would you measure whether DevOps training helped?','Look for demonstrated ability and improved work outcomes in representative tasks, not only course completion. Provide time for practice, mentoring, feedback, and use in the actual workflow, while avoiding attributing every team metric change solely to training.']
];
